How plant genomes accommodate selective pressures that differ in spatial and temporal scales remains poorly understood. Here we address this by comparing the population-genomic signatures of climate- and root microbiome-associated loci in a natural Lotus japonicus population. Inoculating 155 accessions with a synthetic community of 107 bacterial isolates, we identified 355 significant QTLs representing 1,228 unique SNPs associated with rhizosphere microbiome variation. These loci showed heterogeneous population-genomic signatures, contrasting sharply with uniform differentiation-driven patterns at climate-associated loci. One subset of microbiome loci showed near-fixation of specific haplotypes within a restricted geographic region, resembling the pattern seen at climate loci but at a much finer spatial scale. A second subset showed low differentiation, rapid LD decay and maintenance of multiple haplotypes within populations, exemplified by a gene we term ROOT MICROBIOME ESTABLISHMENT 1 (ROOMIE1). Disruption of ROOMIE1 reproducibly altered rhizosphere microbiome composition when plants were grown with synthetic bacterial community and native Japanese soils, and natural ROOMIE1 haplotypes were associated with microbiome differences among related accessions. Together, these findings reveal that microbiome-associated loci can display signatures contrasting sharply with climate-associated loci, with ROOMIE1 representing a host gene that controls rhizosphere microbiome composition and shows standing haplotype variation at local scale.
